
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that settles the substrate, and it immediately sharpens the interesting question.

  In a browser-only, one-user-per-browser app, [3myou[0m are the only one who can change a task. So a notification saying
  "task X changed" would be telling you about something you did two seconds ago. The only changes that genuinely arrive
  without you knowing are [1mtime-based[0m ones — a due date landing, something going overdue.

  That's not a nitpick; it decides what the subsystem actually is. So:

  [1mThe fork:[0m what event source the notification system watches.

  - [1mTime/deadline-driven[0m — a scheduler notices due and overdue tasks and raises notifications. This is the one that has
    real informational value in a single-user app: it tells you something you didn't already know. Costs a
    clock/scheduler concept and a rule for "when do we re-check." Requires tasks to have due dates.
  - [1mMutation-driven ("watch this task")[0m — you mark tasks as watched, and any edit to them raises a notification. Honest
    to your original wording, but in single-user mode it's an activity log with a bell on it. Cheap to build; low value
    now, and it becomes the right design the moment a backend appears.
  - [1mBoth, over one notification store[0m — a single notification list fed by two producers. Costs roughly one extra
    producer plus a [38;5;153mkind[39m field, and the store/UI work is shared, so it's less than double. Slight risk of building the
    mutation half for a multi-user future that may not arrive.

  [1mMy recommendation: time/deadline-driven only[0m, and defer the mutation half. It's the half that earns its keep today,
  and because both feed the same store, adding the mutation producer later is additive rather than a rewrite. This does
  mean tasks need a due-date field, which touches the task model you don't have yet.
